{"candidates":[{"content":"SRC_002 reduces to SRC_003: SRC_002's requirement that the fold operation be commutative (with duplicate suppression) is a generalization of SRC_003's requirement that writers be idempotent. When the fold operation is commutative and duplicate-aware, it achieves exactly the deterministic-replay guarantee that SRC_003 demands at the writer level—but relocates the obligation from writer to consumer. The commutative fold IS the mechanism by which writer idempotency's effect (f(f(x))=f(x)) is achieved downstream. This relation is REFUTED IF there exist event-stream configurations where a commutative, duplicate-suppressing fold yields deterministic replay but where some additional property beyond replay-determinism (e.g., log-size minimality, causal-ordering preservation, storage-cost bounds) still requires writer-level idempotency that commutativity cannot substitute for.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"SRC_003","quote":"An idempotent operation satisfies f(f(x))=f(x): repeated application yields the same result as a single application."},{"block":"SRC_002","quote":"correctness under at-least-once delivery depends on the consumer's fold operation being commutative (or more precisely, idempotent with respect to duplicate events)."}],"neighbours":["SRC_002","SRC_003"],"typicality":0.82},{"content":"SRC_001 contradicts SRC_004: SRC_001 claims the log demands only writer safety (a strictly weaker condition), while SRC_004 claims the log must require writer idempotency because determinism is an outcome property that safety alone cannot guarantee. These are directly opposed on what the log requires of its writers. This relation is REFUTED IF the two positions can be shown to operate at different system boundaries without genuine disagreement—i.e., if SRC_001's 'safety at the writer→log boundary' and SRC_004's 'idempotency for deterministic replay' address non-overlapping correctness concerns such that both can hold simultaneously without logical tension.